  "math-9d": {
    units: { a: "Correlation and the regression line of y on x" },
    paper: "P2",
    qs: [
      { id: "math-9-d49", u: "a", s: "A", m: "2+2+2", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "The table shows the number of hours, \\(x\\), that six students revised and their test scores, \\(y\\).<br>(a) Find Pearson's product-moment correlation coefficient, \\(r\\).<br>(b) Find the equation of the regression line of \\(y\\) on \\(x\\).<br>(c) Use your equation to estimate the score of a student who revised for 9 hours.",
        data: [["\\(x\\) (hours)", "2", "4", "5", "7", "8", "10"], ["\\(y\\) (score)", "48", "55", "61", "68", "74", "82"]],
        rule: "(a) M1 GDC, A1. (b) A1 A1. (c) M1 substitution, A1.",
        pts: ["(a) GDC linear regression (M1) [1]", "\\(r = 0.998\\) (A1) [1]", "(b) \\(y = 4.31x + \\dots\\) (A1) [1]", "\\(y = 4.31x + 38.8\\) (A1) [1]", "(c) \\(4.31(9) + 38.8\\) (M1) [1]", "\\(\\approx 77.6\\) (A1) [1]"] },
      { id: "math-9-d50", u: "a", s: "A", m: "2+2+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "For a set of data, the regression line of \\(y\\) on \\(x\\) is \\(y = 85.2 - 3.1x\\), with \\(r = -0.92\\). The data were collected for \\(5 \\le x \\le 20\\).<br>(a) Describe the correlation between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).<br>(b) Estimate \\(y\\) when \\(x = 12\\).<br>(c) Explain why the line should not be used to estimate \\(y\\) when \\(x = 40\\).",
        rule: "(a) A1 strong, A1 negative. (b) M1 A1. (c) R1 extrapolation.",
        pts: ["(a) strong (A1) [1]", "negative linear correlation (A1) [1]", "(b) \\(85.2 - 3.1(12)\\) (M1) [1]", "\\(= 48.0\\) (A1) [1]", "(c) \\(x = 40\\) is outside the range of the data, so this would be extrapolation and unreliable (R1) [1]"] },
      { id: "math-9-d52", u: "a", s: "A", m: "2+2+2+1", paper: "P2", html: true, t: "The table shows the midday temperature, \\(x\\) °C, and the number of cold drinks sold at a kiosk, \\(y\\), on seven days.<br>(a) Find the value of \\(r\\).<br>(b) Find the equation of the regression line of \\(y\\) on \\(x\\).<br>(c) Estimate the number of drinks sold when the temperature is 25 °C.<br>(d) State, with a reason, whether this shows that higher temperatures cause more drinks to be sold.",
        data: [["\\(x\\) (°C)", "15", "18", "20", "23", "26", "29", "31"], ["\\(y\\)", "120", "135", "160", "172", "190", "215", "230"]],
        rule: "(a) M1 A1. (b) A1 A1. (c) M1 A1. (d) R1: correlation does not imply causation.",
        pts: ["(a) GDC regression (M1) [1]", "\\(r = 0.995\\) (A1) [1]", "(b) gradient 6.82 (A1) [1]", "\\(y = 6.82x + 16.8\\) (A1) [1]", "(c) \\(6.82(25) + 16.8\\) (M1) [1]", "\\(\\approx 187\\) drinks (A1) [1]", "(d) no, correlation does not prove causation; other factors could be involved (R1) [1]"] },
    ],
  },
